export default {"X000078":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Connectedness conventions on the empty space</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000060\"><span>Connected space (Pi-Base convention)</span><code>P000060</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000078\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000078</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000561\"><span>Nonempty space</span><code>P000561</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000562\"><span>Connected space (Stacks convention)</span><code>P000562</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The empty space admits no separation into two inhabited open sets. It satisfies the Pi-Base connectedness condition and fails the nonemptiness condition in the Stacks definition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004S\">Stacks 004S · connected spaces and connected components</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-004S\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/properties/P000036\">Pi-Base connectedness</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000163\">The empty space</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000078\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000078\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000001":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Same topological space, different scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><span class=\"property-context\">None recorded</span></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000001\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000001</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec} k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}(k[\\varepsilon]/(\\varepsilon^2))</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> each have one point; only the first is reduced. The reduction map is a universal homeomorphism.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01IZ\">Stacks 01IZ · reduced schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01IZ\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04DC\">Stacks 04DC · universal homeomorphisms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-04DC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000001\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000001\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000002":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Separated is not Hausdorff</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000034\"><span>Separated</span><code>P000034</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000002\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000002</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000049\"><span>Hausdorff underlying space</span><code>P000049</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The scheme <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb A^1_k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is separated over k. Its generic point lies in every nonempty open, so the Zariski space is not Hausdorff.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KH\">Stacks 01KH · separation axioms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01II\">Stacks 01II · schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01II\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000002\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000002\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000003":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Quasi-affine need not be affine</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000032\"><span>Quasi-affine</span><code>P000032</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000003\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000003</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The punctured affine plane <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\mathbb A^2_k\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is quasi-affine. Its global ring is <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, by intersecting <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k[x,y]_x</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k[x,y]_y</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> in <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The canonical map to its global spectrum omits the origin and is not an isomorphism.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01P5\">Stacks 01P5 · quasi-affine schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01P5\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KX\">Stacks 01KX · the punctured affine plane</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KX\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01IL\">Stacks 01IL · nonaffineness</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01IL\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000003\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000003\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000004":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Quasi-separated need not be separated</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000004\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000004</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000034\"><span>Separated</span><code>P000034</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Glue two copies of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb A^1_k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> along <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> by the identity. Their intersection is affine, and the finite affine cover makes the scheme quasi-separated. On the product of the two charts over k, the diagonal is the affine diagonal with the origin removed, so it is not closed.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KH\">Stacks 01KH · separation axioms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KH\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000004\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000004\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000005":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Connected need not be irreducible</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000029\"><span>Connected</span><code>P000029</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000005\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000005</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000028\"><span>Irreducible</span><code>P000028</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}(k[x,y]/(xy))</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is the union of two closed affine lines meeting at the origin. Each line is connected, hence their union is connected.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004S\">Stacks 004S · connected spaces and their components</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-004S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000005\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000005\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000006":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Reduced need not be normal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000013\"><span>Reduced</span><code>P000013</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000006\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000006</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The local ring at the origin of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}k[t^2,t^3]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is a domain but does not contain the element t, which is integral over it. Thus the cusp is reduced and not normal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033H\">Stacks 033H · normal schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033H\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000006\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000006\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000007":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Finite type need not be finite presentation</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000026\"><span>Of finite type</span><code>P000026</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000007\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000007</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000027\"><span>Of finite presentation</span><code>P000027</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=k[x_1,x_2,\\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=(x_1,x_2,\\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The closed immersion <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}(A/I)\\to\\operatorname{Spec} A</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is of finite type, but I is not finitely generated, so this morphism is not of finite presentation.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01TO\">Stacks 01TO · morphisms of finite presentation</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01TO\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01T0\">Stacks 01T0 · morphisms of finite type</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01T0\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000007\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000007\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000008":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Flat alone need not imply open</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000006\"><span>Flat</span><code>P000006</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000008\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000008</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000040\"><span>Universally open</span><code>P000040</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb Q</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> → <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is flat by localization. Its image is the generic point, which is not open.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01UA\">Stacks 01UA · flat morphisms locally of finite presentation are universally open</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01UA\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000008\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000008\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000009":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Quasi-compact need not be quasi-separated</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000009\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000009</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Glue two copies of Spec k[x₁,x₂,…] along the union of the principal opens D(xᵢ). The two affine charts cover the result, but their intersection is not quasi-compact: for any finite subfamily the prime generated by those variables belongs to an omitted principal open.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KH\">Stacks 01KH · the affine intersection criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01JB\">Stacks 01JB · glueing schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01JB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KL\">Stacks 01KL · absolute witness from two charts</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KL\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000009\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000009\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000060":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A reduced zero-dimensional scheme with a non-Hausdorff Zariski space</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000048\"><span>Krull dimension at most zero</span><code>P000048</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000013\"><span>Reduced</span><code>P000013</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000060\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000060</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000049\"><span>Hausdorff underlying space</span><code>P000049</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000038\"><span>Spectral</span><code>P000038</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000050\"><span>Profinite underlying space</span><code>P000050</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Put <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\prod_{n\\geq 1}\\mathbf F_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. This Boolean ring has only maximal primes. Let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> be the idempotent supported on coordinate <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, and let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\bigcup_{n\\geq1}D(e_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The opens <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(e_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> are disjoint nonempty singletons, so <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is not quasi-compact. Glue two copies of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}(R)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> along <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> by the identity. The two affine charts make the glued scheme quasi-compact, reduced and of dimension zero. Their intersection is <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, so the scheme fails quasi-separatedness. The criterion of Stacks 0CKV then excludes Hausdorffness.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/092F\">Stacks 092F · absolutely flat rings and maximal primes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-092F\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01JB\">Stacks 01JB · gluing schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01JB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CKV\">Stacks 0CKV · separation in dimension zero</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0CKV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000060\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000060\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000010":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Quasi-separated need not be quasi-compact</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000034\"><span>Separated</span><code>P000034</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000049\"><span>Hausdorff underlying space</span><code>P000049</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000010\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000010</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000050\"><span>Profinite underlying space</span><code>P000050</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> An infinite disjoint union of copies of Spec k is separated over Spec k: its diagonal is the union of the diagonal components in a disjoint union of point schemes. The cover by the individual copies has no finite subcover.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KH\">Stacks 01KH · quasi-separated schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01K2\">Stacks 01K2 · quasi-compact schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01K2\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000010\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000010\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000011":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A two-point spectral space that is not profinite</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000056\"><span>Spectral space</span><code>P000056</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000011\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000011</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000057\"><span>T₁ space</span><code>P000057</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000053\"><span>Hausdorff space</span><code>P000053</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000055\"><span>Profinite space</span><code>P000055</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The spectrum of a discrete valuation ring has one generic point and one closed point. It is spectral, but the generic point is not closed. In the patch topology both singletons are open, so that topology is finite discrete.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08YF\">Stacks 08YF · spectral spaces and the constructible topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08YF\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZY\">Stacks 08ZY · profinite spaces</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08ZY\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000010\">π-Base S000010 · Sierpiński space</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000011\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000011\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000012":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A compact Hausdorff space that is not profinite</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000052\"><span>Quasi-compact space</span><code>P000052</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000053\"><span>Hausdorff space</span><code>P000053</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000012\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000012</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000054\"><span>Totally disconnected space</span><code>P000054</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000055\"><span>Profinite space</span><code>P000055</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000056\"><span>Spectral space</span><code>P000056</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The real interval [0,1] is compact and Hausdorff, but connected and has more than one point. It cannot be profinite, since the finite discrete coordinate quotients of a profinite space separate its points.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZY\">Stacks 08ZY · profinite spaces</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08ZY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000012\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000012\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000013":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A Noetherian space with a non-Noetherian structure sheaf</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000066\"><span>Noetherian underlying space</span><code>P000066</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000013\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000013</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000036\"><span>Noetherian</span><code>P000036</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Let A be the square-zero extension of a field k by an infinite-dimensional k-vector space V. Every prime contains V, so Spec A consists of one point and is Noetherian as a space. The ideal V is not finitely generated over A, since the action on V factors through k.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01OU\">Stacks 01OU · Noetherian schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01OU\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0050\">Stacks 0050 · Noetherian spaces</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-0050\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000013\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000013\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000014":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>The p-adic integers are profinite but not extremally disconnected</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000055\"><span>Profinite space</span><code>P000055</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000014\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000014</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000065\"><span>Extremally disconnected space</span><code>P000065</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The p-adic integers are the inverse limit of the finite discrete quotient rings modulo powers of p. The subset of nonzero elements with even p-adic valuation is open. Its closure adds 0, which is not interior because every neighbourhood of 0 also contains elements of odd valuation.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZY\">Stacks 08ZY · profinite spaces</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08ZY\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08YH\">Stacks 08YH · extremally disconnected spaces</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08YH\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000014\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000014\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000077":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A nonflat base change can destroy the local complete intersection property</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000019\"><span>Finite</span><code>P000019</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000023\"><span>Proper</span><code>P000023</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000007\"><span>Unramified</span><code>P000007</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000077\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000077</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000008\"><span>Local complete intersection</span><code>P000008</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000006\"><span>Flat</span><code>P000006</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> be a field and base-change the closed immersion <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>↪</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}k\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{Spec}k[t]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> defined by <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> along <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k[t]\\to k[t]/(t^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The original ideal is generated by a nonzerodivisor. After base change the ideal <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is nonzero and square-zero in the local ring <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k[t]/(t^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Any generating Koszul-regular sequence there would be regular by the Noetherian local criterion. Its first generator would be a nonzerodivisor in <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, which is impossible. Apply the immersion criterion using the identity as the smooth factor.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/069G\">Stacks 069G · criterion for a fixed smooth factorization</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-morphisms/#tag-069G\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09CC\">Stacks 09CC · Koszul regular sequences over local Noetherian rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-09CC\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/068E\">Stacks 068E · local complete intersection morphisms and flat base change</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-morphisms/#tag-068E\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000077\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000077\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>"};
